AB  - Good expenditure performance enhances economic development, growth of a nation and welfare of the citizens. The study assessed the effects of public debt and corruption control on government expenditure performance in Nigeria. The moderating effects of corruption control on the relationship between public debt and government expenditure performance were considered using inflation rate and foreign exchange rate as control variables. The study used longitudinal research design and time series data. Secondary data were used for this study, covering the period of 30 years spanning from the year 1993 to 2022, using the census method. Descriptive statistics and autoregressive distributed lag (ARDL) were conducted. The results found that public debt, inflation rate and average exchange rate have a significant positive impact on government expenditure (F-statistic = 87.93, p-value = 0.0020). Additionally, the moderating effect of corruption control on the relationship between public debt and government expenditure showed a marginally significant association (β = 0.945875; p-value 0.000). The study recommended that the National Assembly, particularly its finance and appropriation committees, should strengthen oversight functions to ensure that debt is managed prudently and sustainably. Also, corruption control measures should be strengthened to enhance fiscal management and improve the efficiency of government spending.
