AB  - The article examines the role of agricultural public spending (APS) and institutional quality (IQ) in food and nutrition security in sub-Saharan Africa. It emphasizes that, although APS can improve food security, their effectiveness is often compromised by low institutional quality, particularly due to corruption and inadequate regulation. The results show that APS have a positive impact on food security only when IQ is sufficiently high. The study concludes that to achieve food security goals, it is essential to simultaneously improve public spending and the quality of institutions.
