AB  - The purpose of this study is to investigate the impact of public expenditures, public employment and public effectiveness on corruption. In this study, which covers the annual data of 33 OECD countries for the period 2007-2021, the data on the corruption perception index, which is the dependent variable, was taken from the official website of Transparency International. Data on the independent variables, the ratio of public expenditures to Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and the share of public employment in total employment, were taken from the OECD official website. The other independent variable, the public effectiveness variable, was obtained from the World Bank official website. As a result of the analysis using the Panel-ARDL method, it was determined that the increase in public effectiveness increased the corruption perception index and thus reduced corruption. In addition, it has been determined that the increase in public employment reduces the corruption perception index and, in this context, increases corruption.
