AB  - Many has regarded misappropriation of assets as innocent because it runs from small misuse of stationeries to misuse of official vehicles for personal interest. Even though the problems may not significant, this could lead to leakages in government expenditure for the authority has spent unnecessary costs to cater this type of crime. The objectives of this study is to find the empirical evidence on awareness against fraud deterrence among Malaysian public servants. Based on Rules no.4.(2)(C) of Peraturan-Peraturan Pegawai Awam (Kelakuan dan Tatatertib) 1993 (Pindaan 2002), this research employed semi-structural interview method to capture details and descriptive data so that this study is able to get the comprehensive understanding on the missing link between government inspiration and practice. 6 participants from a city council have been selected for interviews session while 600 questionnaires were distributed to 99 local authorities to see whether the main pattern from interview analysis still hold. 201 responses from the questionnaires are returned and used. In effect, many have claimed that they are not familiar with Rules no.4(2)(C) even though the policy is available in the government's portal. Instead, their decisions on what is right or wrong are entirely dependable on their faith and internal strength. Hence, this research has shown that there is a missing link between government inspiration and implementation. This research employed phenomenological research method to find the real essence on the issues of misappropriation of assets and corruptions. Even though the studies emphasized on local authorities in Malaysia, the scenario is applicable to all local authorities across the boundaries
