AB  - The scope of government spending has been continuously expanding beyond its traditional functions in many countries. Therefore, it will be useful to clarify the driving forces behind the expanding government spending. The goal of this study is to investigate the impacts of economic and political factors on government spending by using the Turkish annual data for the period of 1980-2012. We use the Engle-Granger cointegration analysis and employ an error correction model to examine the relationship between government spending and economic and political factors, namely GDP growth rate, unemployment rate, trade openness, literacy rate, democracy scores, and corruption. Estimation results indicate that political factors along with economic variables have significant impacts on Turkey's government spending both in the short run and long run.
