AB  - For a sample of 29 European countries, the efficiency of social protection public expenditures in providing income support and diminishing income inequality is assessed in a panel framework. An increase in the size of the government intervention through social protection public expenditure is found to be associated with a decrease in income inequality. Also, we found evidence of the Kuznets effect. Not least, the results pointed out that a lower income inequality could be found in economies with high levels of employment, high tertiary education achievement, low levels of corruption and low levels of old age dependency ratio.
