AB  - The article assesses the Structural Adjustment Program (SAP) of economic recovery adopted by Nigeria in 1986. Nigeria may be achieving a more serious posture for tackling her economic problem. With the return to civilian rule program proceeding smoothly, one of the major obstacles to economic development, namely political instability, is being gradually overcome. It is hoped that the incoming civil government will not only continue the reform program and review it to reflect the recommendations outlined here, but also stick to the core reform measures of reduction in its deficits-reduction of subsidies to public enterprises and parastatals coupled with more privatization and deregulation of State enterprises, wholesome reductions of the size of the public service, and more substantial cuts of the defense budget. Structural re-adjustment must not entail the mere imposition of the provisions of the SAP. It must also be combined with measures designed to avoid the old, familiar economic errors of central decision-making, excessive government spending, over-regulation of business, protectionism, and other uncompetitive rigidities that have tended to mar many an otherwise promising economic policy drive that Nigeria had pursued in the past. More importantly, there must be serious effort to stamp out the socio-economic ills of corruption, mismanagement, and overcome the lack of accountability in public office, which have hitherto plagued Nigeria's geopolitical economy since independence.
